steps.body:
A recursive function calls itself on a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive function needs two parts: a **base case** that answers directly and stops, and a **recursive case** that shrinks the input and calls itself.

```go
func factorial(n int) int {
	if n <= 1 {
		return 1 // base case
	}
	return n * factorial(n-1) // recursive case
}
```

Each call gets its own **stack frame** with its own copy of `n`, so `factorial(5)` waits while `factorial(4)` runs, and so on down to `factorial(1)`. Then the frames return one by one and the products multiply on the way back up.

**Gotcha:** forget the base case and the calls never stop. A Go stack starts small and grows on demand, so the program keeps allocating frames. On your own machine it stops at Go's 1 GB stack limit with `fatal error: stack overflow`. Here the sandbox's 512 MB memory cap kills it first, and the run just shows `signal: killed`. If you see that, check your base case first.

Shrinking by digits works the same way: `n % 10` is the last digit and `n / 10` is the rest of the number.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func factorial(n int) int {
	if n <= 1 {
		return 1
	}
	return n * factorial(n-1)
}

func sumDigits(n int) int {
	if n < 10 {
		return n
	}
	return n%10 + sumDigits(n/10)
}

func main() {
	var n, m int
	fmt.Scan(&n, &m)
	fmt.Printf("factorial(%d) = %d\n", n, factorial(n))
	fmt.Printf("sumDigits(%d) = %d\n", m, sumDigits(m))
}

