steps.body:
Plain recursion can repeat the same work over and over. `fib(n) = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)` computes `fib(n-2)` twice, and the repeats multiply at every level: the naive `fib(40)` makes over 300 million calls.

**Memoization** fixes this by caching each answer the first time you compute it. Use a `map[int]int` from input to result and pass it to every call. Check the cache before doing any work, and store the result before returning.

```go
func fib(n int, memo map[int]int) int {
	if n <= 1 {
		return n
	}
	if v, ok := memo[n]; ok {
		return v // already computed
	}
	memo[n] = fib(n-1, memo) + fib(n-2, memo)
	return memo[n]
}
```

Passing the map is cheap: a map value is a small pointer to one shared table on the heap, so every call reads and writes the same cache. Now each `n` is computed once, so `fib(50)` makes about 100 calls instead of tens of billions.

Remember to create the map with `make` before the first call. Writing to a nil map panics.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func fib(n int, memo map[int]int) int {
	if n <= 1 {
		return n
	}
	if v, ok := memo[n]; ok {
		return v
	}
	memo[n] = fib(n-1, memo) + fib(n-2, memo)
	return memo[n]
}

func main() {
	var n int
	fmt.Scan(&n)
	memo := make(map[int]int)
	fmt.Printf("fib(%d) = %d\n", n, fib(n, memo))
	fmt.Println("cached values:", len(memo))
}

